            <ArticleTitle>Identification of important genes associated with total cholesterol using bioinformatics analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>219-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pgs.15.164</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to identify related genes for total cholesterol (TC) and evaluate the functional relevance to provide evidences for prioritizing these genes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed an initial gene-based association study in about 188,578 individuals. Furthermore, we performed bioinformatics analyses to support the identified genes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 22,098 genes were analyzed for TC levels in gene-based association analysis and 433 of them were found to be significant after Bonferroni correction (p &lt; 2.3 × 10(-6)).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The evidence obtained from the analyses of this study signified the importance of many known genes as well as some novel genes, for example, NR1I2, STARD3 and FN1. The findings might provide more insights into the genetic basis of lipid metabolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
